Now let me look at the `readImage1` function more carefully. It creates a `Format_Mono` image and reads data into it. The `Format_Mono` image uses `malloc` for its data, which doesn't zero-initialize. If the PCX file has fewer bytes per line than expected, some bytes in the scanline might remain uninitialized.

Looking at `readImage1`:
```cpp
img = QImage(header.width(), header.height(), QImage::Format_Mono);
img.setColorCount(2);

for (int y = 0; y < header.height(); ++y) {
    if (s.atEnd()) {
        img = QImage();
        return;
    }

    readLine(s, buf, header);
    uchar *p = img.scanLine(y);
    unsigned int bpl = qMin((quint16)((header.width() + 7) / 8), header.BytesPerLine);
    for (unsigned int x = 0; x < bpl; ++x) {
        p[ x ] = buf[x];
    }
}
```

The `bpl` is the minimum of `(width + 7) / 8` and `header.BytesPerLine`. If `header.BytesPerLine` is larger than `(width + 7) / 8`, only `(width + 7) / 8` bytes are written. But the image's `bytesPerLine()` might be larger than `(width + 7) / 8` due to alignment. So the extra bytes in the scanline would remain uninitialized.

